## Why This Is Asked

Tests a real-world asynchronous export flow requiring idempotency, retry logic, and observability. It validates high-concurrency behavior and failure modes across the message broker and downstream analytics partner.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotent POST endpoints with deduplication keys
- At-least-once vs exactly-once processing semantics
- End-to-end observability and monitoring
- Exponential backoff retry strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example test skeleton for idempotent export
async function testIdempotentExport(exportId) {
  const res1 = await post('/exports', {
    user_id: 'user123',
    export_options: { format: 'csv', date_range: '30d' },
    export_id: exportId
  });

  const res2 = await post('/exports', {
    user_id: 'user123',
    export_options: { format: 'csv', date_range: '30d' },
    export_id: exportId
  });

  // First request should queue the export
  assert(res1.status === 202);
  // Second request should return existing export
  assert(res2.status === 200 && res2.data.export_id === exportId);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates your ability to design comprehensive test strategies for distributed payment systems that must handle real-world complexities including idempotency, cross-service coordination, and failure scenarios.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotency mechanisms for financial operations
- Cross-service correlation and distributed transaction consistency
- Real-time status delivery via SSE/WebSocket protocols
- Resilience patterns including backoff retries and circuit breakers
- Chaos engineering for downstream service failures

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example test for idempotency handling
describe('POST /payments idempotency', () => {
  it('should return same chargeId for duplicate requests', async () => {
    const idempotencyKey = 'test-key-123';
    const request = { amount: 100, currency: 'USD' };

    const firstResponse = await request(app)
      .post('/payments')
      .set('Idempotency-Key', idempotencyKey)
      .send(request);

    const secondResponse = await request(app)
      .post('/payments')
      .set('Idempotency-Key', idempotencyKey)
      .send(request);

    expect(firstResponse.body.chargeId).toBe(secondResponse.body.chargeId);
  });
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses hands-on planning expertise across REST and WebSocket streaming, tenant isolation, contracts, and resilience—areas often under-tested in real-time systems but critical for production reliability.

## Key Concepts

- **Contract Testing**: Validate agreements between gateway and downstream services using PACT or similar frameworks to ensure API compatibility.
- **WebSocket Streaming**: Test connectivity, message ordering, heartbeats, and backpressure handling under load.
- **Per-Tenant Isolation**: Verify data separation through keys, partitions, and role-based access controls to prevent cross-tenant leakage.
- **Fault Injection**: Test circuit breakers, retry logic, and fallback mechanisms when downstream services throttle or fail.
- **Observability**: Implement OpenTelemetry traces, structured logs, and dashboards for visibility into system behavior.
- **End-to-End SLAs**: Validate latency, throughput, and data integrity requirements per tenant.

## Code Example

```java
@Test
void shouldIsolateTenantsInWebSocketStream() {
    String tenantA = "tenant-a-key";
    String tenantB = "tenant-b-key";

    WebSocketClient clientA = new WebSocketClient(wsUrl, tenantA);
    WebSocketClient clientB = new WebSocketClient(wsUrl, tenantB);

    clientA.connect();
    clientB.connect();

    clientA.send("telemetry:update", payloadA);
    clientB.send("telemetry:update", payloadB);

    assertThat(clientA.receive()).doesNotContain(payloadB);
    assertThat(clientB.receive()).doesNotContain(payloadA);
}
```

## Test Cases

| Category | Test Case | Acceptance Criteria |
|----------|-----------|---------------------|
| REST | Concurrent tenant requests | Each tenant receives only their own data |
| WebSocket | Backpressure under high message rate | Messages queue without dropping or timing out |
| Contract | Gateway matches downstream schema | PACT verification passes |
| Resilience | Downstream timeout | Gateway returns 503 within SLA |
| Throttling | Rate limit exceeded | 429 with proper retry-after header |

