## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates practical webhook security testing skills at a beginner level, focusing on cryptographic verification and attack scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- HMAC verification
- Timing-safe comparisons
- Timestamp tolerance
- Replay protection
- Test harness usage

## Code Example

```javascript
// Node.js example
const crypto = require('crypto');
function sign(body, secret){
  return crypto.createHmac('sha256', secret).update(body).digest('hex');
}
function verify(body, ts, provided, secret){
  const expected = sign(body, secret);
  return crypto.timingSafeEqual(Buffer.from(provided,'hex'), Buffer.from(expected,'hex')) && Math.abs(Date.now() - Number(ts)) < 300000;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate replay protection in a distributed environment?
- What changes if the webhook payload is large or binary?

## Why This Is Asked
Introduces an angle not covered: rate-limiting, pagination and input validation in a single endpoint, a common real-world pattern not covered by existing questions.

## Key Concepts
- Input validation and schema contracts
- Rate limiting and Retry-After semantics
- Pagination tokens (cursor) semantics and consistency
- Idempotency and concurrency
- Tooling: Postman, Newman, pytest-requests, k6 for load; mocking rate limiter; contract tests with JSON Schema

## Code Example
